"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[51445],{951445:e=>{e.exports="# Analyze Benchmark Failures\n\n**USE THIS SKILL** when the author asks to:\n\n- \"Analyze this benchmark run and propose fixes to the space context\"\n- \"Diagnose the benchmark failures\"\n- \"Why did these benchmarks fail?\"\n- \"What should I fix to make more benchmarks pass?\"\n\nWhen this skill applies, **immediately start retrieving context**. Do NOT ask clarifying questions before reading the run. Ask only after step 2 if the failures don't cluster cleanly enough to recommend fixes.\n\n## Context\n\nThe author has just run benchmarks (or is looking at a past run) and wants you to surface common failure themes and propose concrete fixes they can accept. The goal is **roll-up across many failures**, not single-message diagnosis. You have full read/write access to the space's tables, instructions, knowledge snippets, columns, and benchmarks.\n\nThe agent's context already includes `## Current Genie Space` with the **ID**. `getBenchmarkResults` resolves the target run automatically: it reads the `runId` (and `evalId` if present) from the current URL when the author is on a benchmark detail page, and falls back to the most recent run otherwise. You do not need to ask the author for a URL or run ID — just call the tool.\n\nThis skill is grounded in the same failure-mode taxonomy as `diagnose-message-failure` (Incorrect SQL Logic, Inconsistency, Misapplied Semantics, Ignored Instructions, Overconfidence, Time-based, Out-of-scope), but applied to a population of failures rather than one message.\n\n### How to read pass/fail (read this before anything else)\n\nThe `getBenchmarkResults` tool already classifies evals for you and returns a summary line of the form `{passed}/{total} passed, {failed} failed, {needs_review} need review`, followed by per-eval details for every non-passing eval (Failed and Needs review combined). Use those counts directly — do not recompute. **Do not paraphrase that summary; if it says `0/1 passed, 0 failed, 1 need review`, do not report `1/1 passed (100% pass rate)`.** A single non-passing eval is still in scope for diagnosis.\n\nFor reference, the classification is driven entirely by `score`:\n\n| Bucket           | Condition                                                  |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Passed**       | `score === 100`                                            |\n| **Failed**       | `score === 0`                                              |\n| **Needs review** | anything else (typically `score === 50`) — **NOT passing** |\n\n`manual_review === true` is orthogonal metadata: it means the score was set by a human override rather than the auto-judge. It does not change the bucket.\n\n**`score === 50` is never passing.** Only evals with `score === 100` count toward the pass rate.\n\nTreat `Failed` and `Needs review` evals together as the population to diagnose. Each per-eval block in the tool output carries a `Status:` line so you can describe them in the author's terms (\"this eval needs review\" vs \"this eval failed\").\n\n## Investigation Steps\n\n### Step 1 — Pull the run results\n\nCall `getBenchmarkResults` with no arguments. The tool resolves scope from the current URL on its own:\n\n- **On a benchmark detail page** — auto-scopes to the run in the URL. If the URL also has `evalId`, the tool returns just that single evaluation (treat as a single-message diagnosis and consider delegating to `diagnose-message-failure` mentally; you can still continue here if the author asked you to).\n- **Anywhere else** — falls back to the most recent run for the space.\n\n**Do not ask the author to paste a URL or run ID.** The tool already has access to the URL via the agent's context, so prompting for one is redundant. If the author later clarifies they wanted a different run, call the tool again with the run ID they provide — but never request it upfront.\n\nThe tool returns markdown formatted like:\n\n```\n## Benchmark Results (run <id>)\n**{passed}/{total} passed**, {failed} failed, {needs_review} need review\n\n### Failed and needs-review questions\n#### 1. \"<question text>\"\n- **Status:** Failed | Needs review (score: …, manual_review: …)\n- **Eval ID:** …\n- **Score reasons:** …\n- **Expected SQL:** …\n- **Generated SQL:** …\n- **Applied context:** { ent_instructions, cited_instruction_line_numbers, problem_analysis, high_level_plan, useful_columns, qn_entities, filter_columns, join_columns, select_columns, useful_values, relevant_instruction_text, relevant_sql_instructions, query_references, assumptions, thinking_process }\n```\n\nUse the summary line verbatim — the tool has already done the classification. Each per-eval block carries its own `Status:` so you can describe failed vs needs-review evals correctly when discussing them.\n\nIf an eval is missing applied context (older runs may not have it), say so explicitly when discussing that eval rather than guessing.\n\n### Step 2 — Cluster failures into themes\n\nCluster the **`Failed` and `Needs review` evals together** — both are surfaces the author needs to address. Group them using **both** signals — score reasons alone are too coarse:\n\n1. **Primary axis: `score_reasons`.** Failures sharing the same dominant score reason are good candidates for the same theme. Common groupings:\n   - `LLM_JUDGE_MISSING_OR_INCORRECT_FILTER` / `LLM_JUDGE_MISSING_OR_INCORRECT_JOIN` / `LLM_JUDGE_INCORRECT_TABLE_OR_FIELD_USAGE` → likely **Misapplied Semantics** or **Ignored Instructions**.\n   - `LLM_JUDGE_INCORRECT_METRIC_CALCULATION` / `LLM_JUDGE_MISSING_OR_INCORRECT_AGGREGATION` → likely **Incorrect SQL Logic**.\n   - `LLM_JUDGE_INSTRUCTION_COMPLIANCE_OR_MISSING_BUSINESS_LOGIC` → likely **Ignored Instructions** or **Inconsistency**.\n   - `LLM_JUDGE_MISINTERPRETATION_OF_USER_REQUEST` → likely **Misapplied Semantics** or **Out-of-scope**.\n   - `EMPTY_RESULT` / `RESULT_MISSING_ROWS` / `RESULT_EXTRA_ROWS` → look at the SQL diff between expected and generated to choose the right mode.\n\n2. **Secondary axis: applied-context signals.** Within a score-reason cluster, refine by:\n   - **Same column or table** appearing in `filter_columns` / `join_columns` / `select_columns` across multiple failures — strong signal for a column-description or synonym fix.\n   - **Same cited instruction line numbers** (`cited_instruction_line_numbers`) across multiple failures where Genie still got it wrong — strong signal that the instruction wording is confusing or being misapplied.\n   - **Same missing entity** — Genie's `qn_entities` repeatedly miss the same business term — strong signal a knowledge snippet or text instruction is needed.\n   - **Same expected-vs-generated SQL diff pattern** — e.g. `COUNT(*)` in generated vs `COUNT(DISTINCT …)` in expected across many failures → standardized measure missing.\n\nAim for **3–6 themes** for a typical run. Fewer if the failures genuinely have one or two root causes; more only if the run is large and diverse. Don't force every failure into a theme — set aside long-tail one-offs as \"individual failures, low pattern signal.\"\n\n### Step 3 — Rank themes by impact\n\nFor each theme, compute:\n\n- **Failure count** — how many failures this theme covers.\n- **Fix confidence** — High if the SQL diff or applied-context pattern is unambiguous; Medium if one of two fixes would work; Low if the theme is \"Genie is confused\" without a specific actionable change.\n- **Fix scope** — local (one column or instruction) vs broad (multiple tables, run-as setting, etc.).\n\nRank themes by `failure count \xd7 fix confidence`. Surface the top themes first; long-tail one-offs go at the bottom (or are dropped).\n\n### Step 4 — Resolve cited instructions and read space context\n\nBefore drafting fixes, complete the picture:\n\n- For instruction line numbers cited across themes (from `cited_instruction_line_numbers` and `relevant_sql_instructions`), call `readInstructions` once for the space and look up the cited bodies. This tells you whether the instruction is poorly worded, conflicting with another, or simply missing.\n- Call `readSpaceConfig` if you don't already have it cached from the session.\n- For each table referenced repeatedly across failures, call `readTableConfig` to see existing column descriptions, synonyms, visibility, and entity-matching state — so you don't propose fixes that duplicate what's already there.\n- If a theme might be addressed by an existing benchmark or curated question, call `readCuratedQuestions` to check.\n\nIssue these reads in parallel where possible.\n\n### Step 5 — Recommend concrete fixes, theme by theme\n\nFor each ranked theme, draft the **actual content** the author would add — not a label of \"I'll add an instruction here.\" Tie each fix to the failure count and the specific tool that will apply it. Examples:\n\n- \"**Theme 1: `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT message_id)` (6 failures).** Add a SQL expression `COUNT(DISTINCT message_id)` named `total_messages_sent`, with synonyms 'total messages', 'message count'. This addresses the 6 failures where the generated SQL used `COUNT(*)` against `events` while the expected SQL used `COUNT(DISTINCT message_id)`.\"\n- \"**Theme 2: open date vs close date (4 failures).** Update column description on `events.opp_date` from '(empty)' to 'Date the opportunity was first opened. NOT the close date — use `cls_date` for close date.' Add synonym `open date` to `opp_date` and synonym `close date` to `cls_date`.\"\n- \"**Theme 3: ignored timezone instruction (3 failures).** The cited instruction (line 12 of the general instructions) says 'use UTC' but Genie's generated SQL is in local time. Rewrite as: 'All timestamp columns are stored in UTC. When the user asks about a day/week/month boundary, wrap the column in `convert_timezone('UTC', 'America/Los_Angeles', …)` before applying `date_trunc`.'\"\n\nGroup fixes by surface (text instructions, SQL examples, knowledge snippets, column metadata, benchmark coverage) so the author can scan and accept the ones they want.\n\nAfter listing fixes, propose a **re-run** so the author can verify the fixes landed: include `[Run benchmarks](#followup)` as a followup link.\n\n## Output Format\n\nThe agent's natural-language commentary while running tools (steps 1–4) serves as the **thinking trace** — briefly describe what was retrieved at each step and why it's relevant. The tool-status pills handle the \"what's running\" part automatically; the trace adds interpretation.\n\nThe **final summary** should follow this structure:\n\n---\n\n**Run summary**\n\n`{passed}/{total} passed`, `{failed}` failed, `{needs_review}` need review. Clustered into `{N}` themes; `{M}` long-tail one-offs. (Only `score === 100` counts as passed — `Needs review` evals are not passing.)\n\n**Themes — review and accept the ones you want**\n\n1. **[Theme title — what's wrong]** _(N failures)_\n\n   _Root cause:_ [2–3 sentences. Reference specific items from the applied context — e.g., \"Across these 6 failures, Genie cited the text instruction 'Currency definition' but the user asked about message counts. No SQL expression exists for `total_messages_sent`, so Genie defaulted to `COUNT(*)`.\"]\n\n   _Proposed fix:_ [Concrete content — the actual instruction text, the actual SQL expression, the actual column description. Written out in full so the author can read and approve without guessing.]\n\n2. **[Theme title]** _(N failures)_\n\n   _Root cause:_ ...\n\n   _Proposed fix:_ ...\n\n3. ...\n\n**Long-tail failures**\n\n- \"[question 1]\" — [one-line description of why it failed; no proposed fix yet]\n- \"[question 2]\" — [one-line description]\n\n> Internal note: keep your failure-mode classification (Incorrect SQL Logic / Inconsistency / Misapplied Semantics / Ignored Instructions / Overconfidence / Time-based / Out-of-scope) as a private mental label that drives which fixes you propose per theme. Do **not** surface the label to the author in the final summary — the plain-language root cause already conveys what went wrong without the jargon.\n\nAfter listing the themes, include `[Run benchmarks](#followup)` as a followup link the author can click after accepting fixes.\n\n---\n\nAfter the summary, wait for the author to indicate which fixes to apply. When they do, call the corresponding edit tools (these all require approval anyway, so the author confirms in the tool dialog). Apply approved fixes in batches when natural — for example a single batched `updateColumnSynonyms` call covering all column-synonym changes for one table, or a single batched `addInstructionsToSpace` call covering several SQL examples.\n\n## Optional Clarifications\n\n- **Themes don't cluster cleanly** — if after step 2 the failures look genuinely heterogeneous (no theme covers ≥3 failures, no shared score reason dominates), say so and ask the author to point you at the failures they care about most. Don't fabricate themes to fill the output.\n\n  Template:\n\n  > \"I read the {N} failed evaluations but they don't cluster into a small set of themes — each one looks like its own issue. Want me to:\n  >\n  > - Walk through the top {3–5} failures one at a time, or\n  > - Focus on a specific failure mode you're most concerned about (wrong calculations, wrong column/filter, ignored instructions, etc.)?\"\n\n- **Run has no failures and nothing needs review** — say so directly and stop. No fixes to propose. Offer to look at the most recent _completed_ run with non-passing evals instead, or suggest running `suggestBenchmarks` / `addBenchmarks` to expand coverage. (If anything is `Needs review`, do not stop here — those are in scope.)\n- **All failures look like one bug** — collapse into one theme rather than padding the list. State the failure count and propose the single fix.\n- **Single-eval scope (URL had `evalId`)** — you'll only get one failure back. Switch to the `diagnose-message-failure` workflow mentally — same skill content applies for a single message — and skip theming since there's nothing to cluster. Use the message's `conversation_id` + `message_id` (returned in the failure detail) if you need to call `getMessageDetails` for richer context.\n\n## Important Notes\n\n- The failure-mode taxonomy is **guidance, not a fixed rule.** Propose the most appropriate fix for the specific theme, even if it differs from the recommendations in `diagnose-message-failure`'s mode list.\n- Never surface internal tool/API identifiers (`addInstructionsToSpace`, `updateColumnSynonyms`, etc.) — describe what will change in the author's terms (\"add a SQL expression…\", \"update the description on column X…\", \"remove this conflicting instruction\"). Internal IDs (instruction IDs, snippet IDs, eval IDs) are likewise not user-facing — quote the human-readable form (question text, instruction title).\n- Always draft the **concrete content** of each proposed change so the author can review the wording before accepting. Don't ask \"Confirm before I apply this?\" — present the actual instruction text, the actual SQL expression, the actual column description.\n- After fixes are applied, offer to re-run benchmarks via `[Run benchmarks](#followup)` so the author can verify. Don't auto-trigger the run.\n- If you can clearly attribute a failure to a single message and the author asks to dig into _that one_, defer to `diagnose-message-failure` for the deep dive — pass it the message_id/conversation_id surfaced by `getBenchmarkResults`.\n"}}]);
